1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,580
I'm going to walk you through what is ChatGPT.

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:06,120
This is the original AI model that everyone knows and loves.

3
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:09,000
So you've probably heard of it, but we're going to walk you through it anyway.

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:11,500
What would be the next word in the sentence?

5
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:14,420
The students open their, what?

6
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:18,860
Could be books, could open their laptops, they could open their exams, their minds.

7
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:23,100
There's a lot of different words that could come next in that sentence.

8
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:27,760
And we can predict what word might come next based on the context in

9
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:29,080
which we're seeing the sentence.

10
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:34,120
And so can ChatGPT, and that is essentially how these models work.

11
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:39,740
They have figured out the probability of what word comes next based on all of the

12
00:00:39,740 --> 00:00:41,860
different words that they've seen on the internet.

13
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:46,040
And, you know, without getting too technical, they have those probabilities

14
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:50,280
baked into the model and they can decide based on the context, based on

15
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:53,100
the prompt that you give them, what word comes next.

16
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:55,460
And they specifically use tokens rather than words.

17
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:59,660
A token is about three fourths of a word on average, a lot of some differences

18
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:01,280
in how they split those words up.

19
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:04,800
But the model is trained until it can predict the next token.

20
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:10,220
And then when it knows on average what the right tokens are to come next in the

21
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:14,000
sequence, then you can give it a new sequence and figure out what comes next

22
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,860
after that and through understanding that through being able to predict the next

23
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:21,720
token, you have to understand quite a lot about how the world works because

24
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:23,500
you need to know how things are associated.

25
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:27,040
And therefore we get the magic that is a large language model.

26
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:31,780
ChancherBT specifically is based on GBT4, which is a transformer model.

27
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:36,060
Transformer models coming from the attention is all you need paper, which

28
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:39,660
is a famous paper, but I'm not going to run you through too much of the jargon

29
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:41,860
here, it won't mean too much to you.

30
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:46,320
Just need to know is that this is, you know, the current kind of state of

31
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,220
the art of this type of model.

32
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:51,760
We don't have another type of model other than transformer models

33
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:53,080
that are doing as good.

34
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:56,720
But, you know, scientists are working on other types of architecture as well.

35
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:58,560
So how did they train ChancherBT?

36
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:02,220
Well, it wasn't just the transformer model that was pretty good on its own.

37
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:06,880
And that's where we had GBT3, but to get ChancherBT to actually kind of make

38
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:09,600
it behave like an assistant, we had to have additional steps.

39
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:14,400
So after training the initial, you know, predict the next token model,

40
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:18,760
the foundation model, then they had to do some fine tuning on top.

41
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:23,960
They would have humans figure out which outputs were best and worst

42
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:27,660
from different questions, and then they would use that to reward the model.

43
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:33,060
So they would predict tokens that humans thought were good answers to questions.

44
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:38,480
Then they had to develop a type of policy reward model, PPO, reinforcement

45
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:42,200
learning, and what that would allow you to do is take a prompt, the sample

46
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:47,240
from the dataset, use that PPO model to generate the output, and then

47
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:51,120
the reward model calculates a reward for the output, and the reward is

48
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:53,200
used to update the policy using PPO.

49
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:56,820
So this is kind of an additional set of reinforcement learning on top.

50
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:02,240
Again, technical jargon, but all that means really is that you have the

51
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:05,900
transformer model that's trained on the internet, then you fine tune it

52
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:11,100
based on what people find helpful and useful in terms of responses, and

53
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:15,180
then you optimize it based on policies so that when you get the results,

54
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:19,680
you can be sure that they're much better on average than what you would

55
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:21,380
get if you just use the foundation model.

